{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And he poured out.</b> As the Targum renders: וְזָרִיז. And likewise ״וַהֲרִיקֹתִי אַחֲרֵיכֶם חָרֶב״ (Leviticus 26) — I will brandish my sword against you; and likewise ״אָרִיק חַרְבִּי״ (Exodus 15); and likewise ״וְהָרֵק חֲנִית וּסְגֹר״ (Psalms 35)." "<b>His trained men.</b> חנכו is the kethib (a variant reads קרי); this is Eliezer, whom he trained for commandments. It is a term of initiation — a person's or a vessel's first entry into the craft in which he is destined to stand, as in ״חֲנֹךְ לַנַּעַר״ (Proverbs 22), ״חֲנֻכַּת הַמִּזְבֵּחַ״ (Numbers 7), ״חֲנֻכַּת הַבַּיִת״ (Psalms 30). In לַעַ\"ז they call it אינצ\"נייר." "<b>Three hundred and eighteen, etc.</b> Our Rabbis said: Eliezer alone was meant (Nedarim 32) — and that is the numerical value (gematria) of his name." "<b>As far as Dan.</b> There his strength failed, for he saw that his descendants were destined to set up an idol there (Sanhedrin 96)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {1 "the la'az is Old French enchenuer or the like, 'to train/initiate'", 3 "Rashi's abbreviation סנה' resolved as Sanhedrin"}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:58:43.181220869Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}